<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      B08 (2026-09-29) · allowBackup was true, which is Android's default and
      the wrong one here. It lets the app's private data — the WebView's cookie
      and storage, which is where the session lives — be pulled off the device
      by `adb backup` or carried to another phone by Google's own transfer.
      This is a business's ledger, its people and its decisions; it should not
      travel except through a sign-in. Nothing in the app depends on backup:
      everything is on the server and a reinstall signs in again.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:exported="true">

            <!--
              PLAY (2026-10-09) · PORTRAIT ON A PHONE. Every phone screen is laid
              out for a tall screen and none for a wide one: turned on its side
              the Desk put the dock over the middle of the page and cut the
              Decisions sheet off below it, with nothing to scroll — which is
              the first thing a reviewer does with a phone. The iOS build was
              set iPhone portrait-only for the same reason (APP_STORE_READINESS
              §2). Android 16 ignores this on screens 600dp and wider, so a
              tablet still rotates, into the desktop layout it was given.
            -->

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              B19 (2026-09-29) · App Links — an invite, a password reset or an
              email verification opens DecisionOS rather than Chrome.

              The paths are listed rather than the whole host on purpose: the
              marketing site, the blog and the PDFs live under the same domain
              and belong to the browser. lib/native/links.js decides the same
              thing again on the JS side, because a link can also arrive while
              the app is already open.

              android:autoVerify asks Android to check
              https://<host>/.well-known/assetlinks.json for this app's signing
              certificate. Until that file is served (frontend/server.js reads
              ANDROID_APP_FINGERPRINT) verification simply fails and links keep
              opening the browser — no crash, and nothing to undo later.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="${appLinkHost}" android:pathPrefix="/login" />
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="${appLinkHost}" android:pathPrefix="/signup" />
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="${appLinkHost}" android:pathPrefix="/reset-password" />
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="${appLinkHost}" android:pathPrefix="/verify-email" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      B02 (2026-09-29) · THE MICROPHONE, WHICH IS MOST OF THE PRODUCT.

      Dex is spoken to: the Desk's ripple, the capture pop-up, the onboarding
      interview, a note on a task. In the APK every one of them answered
      "Microphone not available" the instant it was pressed, and the app never
      appeared under Settings -> Apps -> DecisionOS -> Permissions at all.

      Nothing in the web app was wrong. A webview's getUserMedia arrives at
      Capacitor's BridgeWebChromeClient.onPermissionRequest, which maps
      AUDIO_CAPTURE onto the Android runtime permissions below and launches the
      system prompt. A runtime permission that is not DECLARED here cannot be
      requested: the launcher returns denied immediately, without ever showing
      a dialog, and Capacitor then calls request.deny(). So the founder saw an
      error with no prompt, and no way to fix it in system settings either.

      MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS goes with it because Capacitor asks for both
      together; it is not a runtime permission and raises no prompt of its own.

      Camera is deliberately NOT here. Nothing in the app opens a camera stream
      today — bills are attached through the file picker — and an undeclared
      permission is one the store does not have to be told about.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />

    <!--
      PUSH (2026-10-05) · device notifications (FCM via @capacitor/push-
      notifications). On Android 13+ (this build targets SDK 36) showing a
      notification needs a RUNTIME permission, and like RECORD_AUDIO above it
      cannot be requested unless it is declared here — the plugin's own manifest
      only registers the FCM service, not this permission, so without this line
      PushNotifications.requestPermissions() returns denied with no dialog. The
      FCM token itself works regardless; this is purely about being allowed to
      post the notification to the tray.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
</manifest>
